@node	0.4, 0.5, Chapter 0, Chapter 0
@section Pattern matching and variants

@noindent
Scheme represents structured data with pairs and tags them by convention;
OCaml has a dedicated construct for exactly this, the @newterm{variant}
type, and a dedicated way to take one apart, @code{match}. A variant lists
its possible shapes, each with a name (a @newterm{constructor}) and the data
it carries:

@example
# type shape =
    | Circle of float
    | Rectangle of float * float;;
type shape = Circle of float | Rectangle of float * float
@end example

@noindent
A @code{shape} is either a @code{Circle} carrying one @code{float} (its
radius) or a @code{Rectangle} carrying two (its width and height); nothing
else is a @code{shape}. @code{match} dispatches on the constructor actually
present, binding the data it carries:

@example
# let area = function
    | Circle r -> Float.pi *. r *. r
    | Rectangle (w, h) -> w *. h;;
val area : shape -> float = <fun>
# area (Circle 2.0);;
- : float = 12.5663706143591725
# area (Rectangle (3.0, 4.0));;
- : float = 12.
@end example

@noindent
(@code{function} is shorthand for @code{fun x -> match x with}, useful for a
function whose whole body is one pattern match.) The compiler checks that a
@code{match} covers every constructor of the type it matches on, under the
warning that this edition's build treats as an error; add a constructor to
@code{shape} without adding an arm to @code{area}, and the build stops,
naming @code{area} as the site that no longer covers every case. Exercise
0.3 makes this literal: it asks for a @code{Triangle} constructor added
after the fact, and for the resulting compiler message read as a list of
every site the change touched. In a codebase of any size, that list is the
entire benefit of exhaustiveness checking: representing a new case correctly
becomes a compile error to fix, not a bug to discover at runtime in whichever
branch happens to run.

@subsubheading Guards, and Scheme's @code{cond}

A @code{match} arm may add a @code{when} clause, narrowing which values of a
pattern it accepts; this is this edition's translation of Scheme's
@code{cond}:

@example
# let abs_value n =
    match n with
    | n when n < 0 -> - n
    | n -> n;;
val abs_value : int -> int = <fun>
# abs_value (-4);;
- : int = 4
# abs_value 7;;
- : int = 7
@end example

@subsubheading @code{option}: no more, no less, than ``maybe nothing''

The standard library's own most useful variant is @code{option}, with two
constructors, @code{None} and @code{Some @var{value}}. It replaces every use
in Scheme of a sentinel value (an out-of-band @code{#f}, or a specific
number that ``really'' means absent) with a type the compiler forces the
caller to handle:

@example
# let head = function
    | [] -> None
    | first :: _ -> Some first;;
val head : 'a list -> 'a option = <fun>
# head [ 3; 5 ];;
- : int option = Some 3
# head ([] : int list);;
- : int option = None
# let safe_divide n d = if d = 0 then None else Some (n / d);;
val safe_divide : int -> int -> int option = <fun>
# safe_divide 10 2;;
- : int option = Some 5
# safe_divide 10 0;;
- : int option = None
@end example

@noindent
@code{head}'s type, @code{'a list -> 'a option}, is @newterm{polymorphic}:
the @code{'a} is a type variable, meaning @code{head} works on a list of any
one element type and returns an option of that same type: the function never inspects what kind of value it holds, only whether the list is empty.
A caller of @code{safe_divide} cannot forget to check for division by zero
the way a caller ignoring a Scheme error condition can; the result is an
@code{int option}, not an @code{int}, and getting an @code{int} back out
requires a @code{match} (or one of the combinators section 0.8 introduces)
that has a case for @code{None}.

@subsubheading Building this book's own data with variants

The rest of this book represents its own recursive data (lists, trees, symbolic expressions) with variants built from the same two pieces just
shown: a constructor per shape, and recursion in the type itself where a
shape contains more of the same type. A minimal integer list, reimplemented
by hand as a warm-up for the library list type the next section introduces:

@example
# type int_list =
    | Nil
    | Cons of int * int_list;;
type int_list = Nil | Cons of int * int_list
# let rec total = function
    | Nil -> 0
    | Cons (head, tail) -> head + total tail;;
val total : int_list -> int = <fun>
# total (Cons (1, Cons (2, Nil)));;
- : int = 3
@end example

@noindent
and a small binary tree, in the same shape this book's later chapters use
for symbolic expressions and register-machine instructions:

@example
# type tree =
    | Leaf
    | Node of tree * int * tree;;
type tree = Leaf | Node of tree * int * tree
# let rec tree_sum = function
    | Leaf -> 0
    | Node (left, label, right) -> tree_sum left + label + tree_sum right;;
val tree_sum : tree -> int = <fun>
# tree_sum (Node (Node (Leaf, 1, Leaf), 6, Node (Leaf, 4, Leaf)));;
- : int = 11
@end example

@noindent
Both @code{total} and @code{tree_sum} recurse on the same shape their type
recurses on, and both are exhaustive: @code{Nil}/@code{Leaf} is the base
case, and the other constructor is the recursive case. This is the pattern
every recursive function over this book's own data structures follows, from
chapter 2's hierarchical data onward.

